libretro: the GBA save is always 128 KB, whatever type the game turned out to use

The core keeps every GBA save in one 128 KB buffer (0xFF, with the frontend's file copied in), but reports its size
to the frontend as 128 KB only while the save type is still undetected and as the detected type's size afterwards.
A type is detected from what the game touches: Flash starts as 64 KB and becomes 128 KB only when the game selects
its second bank, EEPROM starts as 512 bytes and becomes 8 KB only when the game writes past them. RetroArch asks for
the size afresh when it writes the save at exit, so a 128 KB Flash save played by a session that stayed in bank 0
came back as 64 KB with bank 1 gone from the file, and an 8 KB EEPROM save by a session that stayed in its first
512 bytes came back as 512 bytes (measured on RetroArch 1.22.2 with small test ROMs; a game in mGBA's override list
has its type from the start and isn't affected). RetroArch's autosave thread, which takes the size once at load,
already writes 128 KB all session; gpSP, the other GBA core, writes 128 KB always. This makes the exit write the
same: 128 KB for every type, with the padding past the game's data 0xFF as gpSP pads it, so the two cores write the
same file for the same game and nothing is cut. A game known to have no save (FORCE_NONE) still gets none.

BananaGBA is libretro's mGBA with the handhelds' changes, the same lines here as in libretro-mgba's copy of this
patch (buildroot/package/libretro-mgba), 55 lines further down.

--- a/src/platform/libretro/libretro.c
+++ b/src/platform/libretro/libretro.c
@@ -2364,10 +2364,10 @@
 #ifdef M_CORE_GBA
 		case mPLATFORM_GBA:
 			switch (((struct GBA*) core->board)->memory.savedata.type) {
-			case GBA_SAVEDATA_AUTODETECT:
-				return GBA_SIZE_FLASH1M;
+			case GBA_SAVEDATA_FORCE_NONE:
+				return 0;
 			default:
-				return GBASavedataSize(&((struct GBA*) core->board)->memory.savedata);
+				return GBA_SIZE_FLASH1M;
 			}
 #endif
 #ifdef M_CORE_GB
